Subject: Include <poll.h> only when the C library provides it

What fails: include/internal/sockets.h includes <poll.h> on every
OPENSSL_SYS_UNIX target. The AROS SDKs used here (ABIv11 SDK, ABIv11
2026.09 SDK, mainline x86_64 SDK) have no <poll.h>, so most of libcrypto
fails with "poll.h: No such file or directory".

Why this fix: the only poll() users in the libraries (crypto/bio/bio_sock.c
BIO_socket_wait, ssl/quic/quic_reactor.c, ssl/rio/*) already fall back to
select() when POLLIN is undefined. So the include is the only problem, and
the condition asks the compiler whether the header exists rather than
naming a system: behaviour is unchanged wherever <poll.h> exists, and a
compiler without __has_include keeps the old behaviour. The check is
nested (#if defined(__has_include) / #if __has_include(...)) because a
preprocessor without the feature cannot parse the call even in a branch
that && would short-circuit.

Scope: all targets; effective only where <poll.h> is absent. Built for
x86_64 AROS ABIv11 (GCC 10.5.0, no-dgram, i.e. without QUIC); the select()
fallback itself is not exercised by aros-syncthing, which supplies its own
socket BIO.

Verification: with this patch libcrypto/libssl compile for ABIv11 (GCC
10.5.0) and the probe links with no undefined symbols; on the macOS host
(clang) the header still includes <poll.h> (POLLIN defined). The branch
for a compiler without __has_include was not compiled. Mainline contrib carries an equivalent
change guarded by OPENSSL_SYS_AROS (openssl-4.0.1-aros.diff).

Status: upstream candidate (to OpenSSL), not yet proposed. Not checked
against 3.5.
---
--- a/include/internal/sockets.h
+++ b/include/internal/sockets.h
@@ -118,7 +118,21 @@
 #endif
 
 #ifdef OPENSSL_SYS_UNIX
-#ifndef OPENSSL_SYS_TANDEM
+/*
+ * Some Unix-like C libraries (AROS) provide no <poll.h>. Ask the compiler
+ * whether the header exists instead of naming systems; without it POLLIN
+ * stays undefined and the poll() users fall back to select(). The test is
+ * nested: a preprocessor without __has_include must not even see the
+ * __has_include(<...>) expression.
+ */
+#if defined(__has_include)
+#if __has_include(<poll.h>)
+#define OSSL_INTERNAL_HAVE_POLL_H
+#endif
+#else
+#define OSSL_INTERNAL_HAVE_POLL_H
+#endif
+#if !defined(OPENSSL_SYS_TANDEM) && defined(OSSL_INTERNAL_HAVE_POLL_H)
 #include <poll.h>
 #endif
 #include <errno.h>
